# openccu-lite: the job behind occu-interface-clock.timer.
#
# Unconditional, on every product (maintainer, 2026-09-06). The review
# proposed gating this on a configured LAN gateway like the two S58/S59 units; the maintainer
# reversed that. /bin/SetInterfaceClock is a closed eQ-3 binary from the RFD package and nobody
# has watched it on a live box: rfd may well pass the time to the built-in radio module as well
# as to a LAN gateway. One XML-RPC call four times a day costs nothing and rfd decides for
# itself when there is nothing to update, so the condition would only risk a class of bug that
# shows up as wrong timestamps days later. The only condition kept is upstream's own: the
# crontab line does not run when the box is itself a LAN gateway.
#
# SetInterfaceClock talks to rfd alone (127.0.0.1:32001); hmipserver answers its devices' time
# requests itself. So the job runs only while rfd is active: on a box without BidCos (an HmIP-only
# stick, HmIP straight on the module) or with rfd down, the run is skipped - an ExecCondition= that
# fails is a skip, not a failed unit that leaves the system degraded. rfd's plan marker is not the
# condition: it says rfd is planned, not that it runs.
[Unit]
Description=Interface clock
# The timer can fire during boot: a board without a real-time clock starts at an old date, the
# first time step jumps past one of the four marks, and the job then ran before rfd and
# hmipserver listened ("write error (error 111)", a failed unit at every such boot). Ordered after
# the interface daemons, a job started in the same boot waits for them.
After=rfd.service hmipserver.service hs485d.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
ExecCondition=/usr/bin/systemctl is-active --quiet rfd.service
ExecStart=/bin/SetInterfaceClock
